from tnsoc import *
S(9,19,"Asha's class elected a leader by voting. \"Is a country run the same way?\" she asked.",
 ["A government is the body that runs a country. Forms of government include monarchy, where a king or queen rules, and democracy, where the people rule through their representatives. Democracy may be direct, as in ancient Athens, or representative, as in India.",
  "Democracy rests on equality, liberty, rule of law and the participation of people. Its features are free elections, rights for all and a government that answers to the people. India is the world's largest democracy.",
  "Democracy has strengths such as accountability and respect for rights, but it needs informed citizens who vote and take part."],
 ["government","monarchy","democracy","representative democracy","rule of law","citizen"],
 ("Why is India a representative democracy?",["Too many people to decide directly.","People elect representatives.","They make laws for all."]),
 ("In a monarchy the people elect the ruler.",["A monarch inherits the throne.","Elections belong to democracy."]),
 ["Democracy is rule by the people.","India is representative.","Rule of law applies to all.","Citizens must take part."],
 ["m|Rule by the people is|democracy|monarchy|dictatorship|People decide.","t|India is the largest democracy.|T|Yes.","m|Democracy in which people elect representatives is|representative|direct|royal|Like India.","t|In a monarchy the ruler is elected.|F|Usually inherited.","m|Equality before law is|rule of law|monarchy|veto|Applies to all.","s|Ancient democracy was in which Greek city?|athens|Direct democracy."])
S(9,20,"Raju watched people line up outside a polling booth. \"What happens after voting?\" he asked.",
 ["Elections let people choose their representatives. In India every citizen aged 18 or above can vote, which is called universal adult franchise. The Election Commission conducts free and fair elections, and electronic voting machines record votes.",
  "Political parties put forward candidates and programmes. They may be national or state parties, and the winning party or group forms the government while the others form the opposition. Coalition governments occur when no party has a majority.",
  "Pressure groups, such as trade unions and farmers' associations, try to influence government decisions but do not contest elections."],
 ["election","franchise","Election Commission","political party","opposition","pressure group"],
 ("Who can vote in India?",["Every citizen aged 18 or above.","This is universal adult franchise.","Regardless of caste, gender or wealth."]),
 ("Pressure groups contest elections to form the government.",["They only influence decisions.","Parties contest elections."]),
 ["Voting age is 18.","Election Commission conducts polls.","Parties form government or opposition.","Pressure groups influence policy."],
 ["m|The voting age in India is|18|21|16|Adult franchise.","t|The Election Commission conducts elections.|T|Yes.","m|Parties other than the ruling one form the|opposition|cabinet|court|Watch the government.","t|Pressure groups form the government.|F|They influence it.","m|Voting by all adults is|universal franchise|monarchy|veto|Equal vote.","s|Machines used for voting are called?|evm|Electronic voting machines."])
S(9,21,"Meena read that every child has a right to education. \"Who gave us rights?\" she asked.",
 ["Human rights are the basic rights every person has simply by being human, such as life, liberty and equality. The United Nations adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights on 10 December 1948, and we mark it as Human Rights Day.",
  "In India the Constitution gives Fundamental Rights, including equality, freedom, protection against exploitation, freedom of religion, cultural and educational rights and the right to constitutional remedies. Children also have rights to education and protection.",
  "Rights come with duties. The National and State Human Rights Commissions look into violations, and courts protect rights."],
 ["human rights","UDHR","Fundamental Rights","exploitation","Human Rights Commission","duties"],
 ("Why do rights come with duties?",["My rights depend on others respecting them.","I must respect others' rights too.","Society works on mutual respect."]),
 ("Human rights were declared by India in 1948.",["The United Nations adopted them.","India's Constitution gives Fundamental Rights."]),
 ["UDHR adopted in 1948.","Human Rights Day is 10 December.","Fundamental Rights are in the Constitution.","Rights come with duties."],
 ["m|Human Rights Day is on|10 December|26 January|15 August|UDHR.","t|The right to education is a child's right.|T|Yes.","m|The UDHR was adopted by the|United Nations|Parliament|Supreme Court|In 1948.","t|Rights have no duties.|F|Duties go with them.","m|The body looking into violations is the|Human Rights Commission|Army|Bank|Protects rights.","s|Which year was the UDHR adopted?|1948|Dec 1948."])
S(9,22,"Asha asked whether all countries have the same government. \"No,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Governments are of different forms. In a unitary government, power rests with the central government, as in the United Kingdom, while in a federal government power is divided between the centre and the states, as in India and the USA.",
  "Governments may also be parliamentary or presidential. In the parliamentary system, as in India, the executive is responsible to the legislature. In the presidential system, as in the USA, the President is elected separately and is both head of state and head of government.",
  "The three organs of government are the legislature that makes laws, the executive that carries them out and the judiciary that interprets them."],
 ["unitary","federal","parliamentary","presidential","legislature","judiciary"],
 ("What is the difference between unitary and federal government?",["In unitary, power is with the centre.","In federal, power is shared.","India is federal."]),
 ("The judiciary makes laws.",["The legislature makes laws.","The judiciary interprets them."]),
 ["Unitary: central power.","Federal: shared power.","Parliamentary vs presidential.","Three organs of government."],
 ["m|India's government is|federal|unitary only|monarchy|Centre and states.","t|The legislature makes laws.|T|Yes.","m|The USA has a|presidential system|monarchy|unitary system|President elected.","t|The executive interprets laws.|F|The judiciary does.","m|The executive in India is responsible to the|legislature|army|press|Parliamentary system.","s|How many organs does government have?|3|Legislature, executive, judiciary."])
S(9,23,"Raju's village had a meeting about a new well. \"Who decides these things?\" he asked.",
 ["Local self-government means people manage local matters through elected bodies. In villages it is the Panchayat Raj, with the gram panchayat at the village level, panchayat union at the block and district panchayat above them. In towns we have municipalities and corporations.",
  "The 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendments of 1992 gave these bodies constitutional status. Seats are reserved for women and for Scheduled Castes and Tribes, and elections are held every five years.",
  "Local bodies look after water supply, roads, street lights, sanitation and schools. The gram sabha, the meeting of all adult villagers, lets people take part directly."],
 ["local self-government","gram panchayat","gram sabha","municipality","73rd Amendment","reservation"],
 ("Why is the gram sabha important?",["All adult villagers can attend.","They discuss and decide village matters.","It keeps the panchayat accountable."]),
 ("Panchayats are only for towns.",["Panchayats are for villages.","Towns have municipalities."]),
 ["Panchayat Raj is rural.","Municipalities are urban.","73rd and 74th Amendments 1992.","Gram sabha: all adult villagers."],
 ["m|The village body is the|gram panchayat|municipality|high court|Rural.","t|Seats are reserved for women.|T|Yes.","m|Urban local bodies are|municipalities|panchayats|courts|For towns.","t|The gram sabha has only the sarpanch.|F|All adult villagers.","m|Constitutional status came in|1992|1950|2005|73rd and 74th.","s|How often are local elections held?|5|Every five years."])
S(9,24,"Meena saw a road sign near her school. \"Why must we obey signs?\" she asked.",
 ["Road safety means following rules to prevent accidents. Many accidents are caused by overspeeding, drunk driving, not wearing helmets or seat belts and breaking signals.",
  "Road signs are of three kinds: mandatory (orders, such as stop), cautionary (warnings, such as a school ahead) and informatory (information such as a hospital). Traffic lights have red for stop, yellow for get ready and green for go.",
  "Pedestrians should use footpaths and zebra crossings, drivers must wear helmets or seat belts and keep to the speed limit. Learning first aid helps in an emergency, and the Motor Vehicles Act sets penalties."],
 ["road safety","mandatory sign","cautionary sign","zebra crossing","helmet","seat belt"],
 ("What do traffic lights tell us?",["Red means stop.","Yellow means get ready.","Green means go."]),
 ("Helmets are not needed for two-wheelers.",["They save lives.","They are required by law."]),
 ["Obey road signs.","Three kinds of signs.","Use zebra crossings.","Wear helmets and seat belts."],
 ["m|Red light means|stop|go|slow|Rule.","t|Seat belts save lives.|T|Yes.","m|A warning sign is|cautionary|mandatory|informatory|Warns of danger.","t|Overspeeding is safe.|F|It causes accidents.","m|Pedestrians should cross at|zebra crossing|anywhere|highway|Safe place.","s|Which light means go?|green|Traffic light."])
S(9,25,"Asha asked why some countries seem richer than others. \"That is development,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Development means improvement in people's lives. It is not only growth in income but also better health, education and living conditions. Different people may have different ideas of development.",
  "Per capita income is the total income divided by the population. The Human Development Index (HDI) measures life expectancy, education and income, and countries are ranked by it. The Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the value of goods and services produced.",
  "Sustainable development meets present needs without damaging the resources for the future. The Sustainable Development Goals set by the UN include ending poverty and hunger, and giving quality education."],
 ["development","per capita income","HDI","GDP","sustainability","SDG"],
 ("What does the HDI measure?",["Life expectancy.","Education.","Income."]),
 ("Development means only more income.",["It also means health and education.","Income is just one part."]),
 ["Development is better living.","HDI: health, education, income.","Per capita income = income / population.","Sustainable development protects the future."],
 ["m|HDI includes|health, education, income|only income|only roads|Three parts.","t|GDP is the value of goods and services.|T|Yes.","m|Per capita income is income divided by|population|area|rainfall|Per person.","t|Development means only income.|F|Also health and education.","m|SDG stands for Sustainable Development|Goals|Groups|Grants|UN goals.","s|HDI includes life expectancy, education and what?|income|Third part."])
S(9,26,"Raju asked his uncle what he did for a living. \"I work in a factory,\" he said.",
 ["Employment means working to earn a living. The economy has three sectors: the primary sector (farming, mining), the secondary sector (industry) and the tertiary sector (services). Workers may be in the organised sector with fixed pay and benefits or the unorganised sector without them.",
  "Unemployment occurs when people who want work cannot find it. Types include disguised unemployment, where more people work on farms than needed, seasonal unemployment and educated unemployment.",
  "Tamil Nadu has a strong industrial base and services sector, with textiles, automobiles and IT. Schemes such as MGNREGA guarantee 100 days of work in rural areas."],
 ["employment","primary, secondary, tertiary","organised sector","unemployment","disguised unemployment","MGNREGA"],
 ("What is disguised unemployment?",["More workers than needed on a farm.","Some add nothing to output.","They seem employed but are not needed."]),
 ("Farming is a tertiary activity.",["Farming is primary.","Services are tertiary."]),
 ["Three sectors.","Organised and unorganised.","Unemployment has types.","MGNREGA gives rural work."],
 ["m|Farming is in the|primary sector|secondary sector|tertiary sector|Natural products.","t|Services form the tertiary sector.|T|Yes.","m|MGNREGA guarantees|100 days of work|a house|free food|Rural scheme.","t|Unorganised workers have fixed benefits.|F|They lack them.","m|Industry is in the|secondary sector|primary|tertiary|Manufacturing.","s|How many sectors does the economy have?|3|Primary, secondary, tertiary."])
S(9,27,"Meena saved coins in a piggy bank. \"Where do banks keep people's money?\" she asked.",
 ["Money is anything accepted for payment. In early times people used barter, exchanging goods, which needed a double coincidence of wants. Money solved this problem. Today we use coins, notes and digital payments.",
  "Banks keep savings safe and give loans. The Reserve Bank of India issues currency and controls the banking system. Banks pay interest on deposits and charge a higher interest on loans.",
  "Credit means borrowing money, which can help, for example to start a business, but too much debt is risky. Self-help groups and cooperative societies give small loans to the poor, and in Tamil Nadu women's SHGs are strong."],
 ["barter","money","Reserve Bank of India","interest","credit","self-help group"],
 ("Why did money replace barter?",["Barter needed two people to want each other's goods.","Money is accepted by all.","It is easy to store and carry."]),
 ("The RBI gives loans to individuals.",["It issues currency and regulates banks.","Banks give loans."]),
 ["Barter is exchange of goods.","Money is a medium of exchange.","Banks give loans and take deposits.","RBI issues currency."],
 ["m|Exchange of goods without money is|barter|credit|tax|Old system.","t|Banks pay interest on deposits.|T|Yes.","m|Currency is issued by the|RBI|SBI|Parliament|Central bank.","t|Credit means saving money.|F|It means borrowing.","m|A group of women saving and lending is a|self-help group|company|court|SHG.","s|What is the central bank of India?|rbi|Reserve Bank."])
S(9,28,"Raju saw paddy fields on the way to his village. \"What do farmers grow here?\" he asked.",
 ["Agriculture is the main occupation in Tamil Nadu. Rice is the chief food crop, and other crops include millets, pulses, sugarcane, cotton, groundnut and coconut. Nutmeg, tea and coffee are grown in the hills.",
  "Water comes from rivers like the Cauvery, from tanks, canals and wells, and the delta region is the rice bowl. Farming depends on monsoon rain, and the Kuruvai, Samba and Thaladi seasons are known in Tamil Nadu.",
  "Farmers face problems such as erratic rain, low prices and debt. Modern methods, drip irrigation, crop insurance and organic farming help, and the Green Revolution raised yields."],
 ["agriculture","Cauvery delta","Samba","drip irrigation","organic farming","Green Revolution"],
 ("Why is the Cauvery delta called the rice bowl?",["It has fertile soil.","The river gives water.","Rice is grown in large amounts."]),
 ("Tamil Nadu agriculture does not depend on rain.",["It depends on the monsoon and rivers.","Rain is important."]),
 ["Rice is the chief crop.","Cauvery delta is the rice bowl.","Drip irrigation saves water.","Farmers face debt and price problems."],
 ["m|The chief food crop of Tamil Nadu is|rice|wheat|tea|Paddy.","t|The Cauvery delta grows rice.|T|Yes.","m|Saving water by dripping at roots is|drip irrigation|flood irrigation|rain|Efficient.","t|Farmers never face price problems.|F|They do.","m|The Green Revolution raised|yields|taxes|rain|Crop output.","s|Which river feeds the delta?|cauvery|The Cauvery."])
S(9,29,"Asha's friend moved to a big city for her father's job. \"Why do people move?\" she asked.",
 ["Migration is the movement of people from one place to another to live. It may be internal, within a country, or international, between countries. It may be permanent or temporary and voluntary or forced.",
  "Push factors drive people away, such as poverty, lack of jobs, drought or war. Pull factors attract them, such as jobs, better schools and safety. Rural to urban migration is common in India.",
  "Migration brings remittances and skills but can cause crowded cities, slums and pressure on services. Migrant workers need protection and good living conditions."],
 ["migration","push factor","pull factor","internal migration","remittance","urbanisation"],
 ("Why do people migrate from villages?",["Few jobs and low income.","Cities offer jobs.","This is the push and pull of migration."]),
 ("Migration happens only between countries.",["It can be within a country too.","That is internal migration."]),
 ["Migration is movement of people.","Push and pull factors.","Rural to urban is common.","It brings money but also problems."],
 ["m|Poverty that drives people away is a|push factor|pull factor|tax|Push.","t|Jobs in cities are a pull factor.|T|Yes.","m|Movement within a country is|internal migration|emigration|trade|Domestic.","t|Migration has no effects on cities.|F|It crowds them.","m|Money sent home by migrants is|remittance|tax|loan|Support.","s|Rural to urban migration leads to growth of?|cities|Urbanisation."])
